<Base
  title="sempods concepts — graph retrieval, federated identity and pod permissions"
  description="Technical concepts behind sempods: graph retrieval, find, Linked Open Data, federated identity, context permissions, MCP and Linked Data CRUD."
  schemaType="TechArticle"
>
  <p class="eyebrow">concepts</p>
  <h1>Technical depth without turning the site into a manual</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    The home page should stay readable. This section is for the person who wants
    to know whether the architecture holds up: how an app or agent finds useful
    data, follows links, respects permissions and keeps working across pods.
  </p>

  <div class="concept-list">
    <article>
      <h2><a href="/concepts/graph-retrieval">Graph retrieval</a></h2>
      <p>
        The first deep dive: why retrieval starts with resources and links, not
        document chunks, and why <code>find</code> is the entry point rather
        than the whole search story.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article>
      <h2><a href="/concepts/federated-grants">Linked Open Data and federated grants</a></h2>
      <p>
        WebIDs, app DIDs, aliases and grants are the identity side of the model:
        apps should connect to pods without every product becoming the account
        system.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article>
      <h2><a href="/concepts/context-permissions">Context permissions</a></h2>
      <p>
        Contexts are the current permission boundary in the reference
        implementation. The authorization model is still being aligned with more
        expressive work such as ACP, but contexts remain the useful way to talk
        about visibility.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article>
      <h2><a href="/concepts/mcp-over-pods">MCP over pods</a></h2>
      <p>
        MCP is another client surface over the same pod rules. Multi-pod MCP is
        interesting because an agent can query independent data spaces instead
        of one central memory service.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article>
      <h2><a href="/concepts/linked-data-crud">Linked Data CRUD</a></h2>
      <p>
        CRUD sounds ordinary because the web is ordinary. That is the point:
        resources can be addressed, read and written over HTTP instead of being
        hidden behind one SDK.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <p class="action-links">
    <a href="/concepts/graph-retrieval">Read graph retrieval</a>
    <a href="/concepts/federated-grants">Read federated grants</a>
    <a href="/how-it-works">Back to the model</a>
  </p>
</Base>
